# E8, the largest exceptional Lie algebra, the top of the symmetry ladder, modeled by the kernel. E8 is a simple Lie
# algebra of rank eight and dimension two hundred forty-eight. Its defining data is its DYNKIN DIAGRAM: eight nodes,
# seven of them in a straight chain and one extra node hung off the third node from one end, the unique shape that the
# classification of simple Lie algebras allows at this size. From the diagram comes the CARTAN MATRIX, the eight-by-eight
# integer matrix A with a two on every diagonal entry, a minus one for each pair of nodes joined by an edge, and a zero
# for each pair not joined. E8 is SIMPLY LACED, every edge is single, so the matrix is symmetric. Its two hundred forty
# ROOTS split into two families: one hundred twelve integer-vector roots, the vectors plus-or-minus e_i plus-or-minus e_j
# in eight dimensions, exactly the roots of D8, and one hundred twenty-eight half-integer spinor roots, one half times
# eight signs with an even number of minus signs. Those two hundred forty roots together with the origin generate the E8
# LATTICE, the unique even self-dual lattice in eight dimensions and the densest lattice packing of spheres known in its
# dimension. E8 is exceptional, the end of the line beyond the infinite A, B, C, D families, and the same root system,
# coarsened, is where the reversible tower of the substrate finally closes.
#
# This file CONSTRUCTS the diagram (the eight nodes and their adjacency table) and the Cartan matrix, and PROVES their
# combinatorial laws by kernel induction over the nodes (symmetry, no self-loops, the diagonal of two). The two root
# families are modeled honestly: the integer-root family is given a real computable predicate (a vector is a D8 root when
# exactly two of its eight coordinates are nonzero), and the count two hundred forty is stated as an arithmetic fact, NOT
# dressed up as a derivation.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# THE DYNKIN NODES. The eight simple roots of E8, one per node of the diagram. The labeling fixes the shape: n1, n2, n3,
# n4, n5, n6, n7 form the long chain in that order, and n8 is the extra node attached to n5. So the edges are the six
# chain bonds n1-n2, n2-n3, n3-n4, n4-n5, n5-n6, n6-n7, plus the branch bond n5-n8: seven edges over eight nodes, which
# makes the diagram a tree.
form node
  case n1
  case n2
  case n3
  case n4
  case n5
  case n6
  case n7
  case n8

# Node equality, used by the adjacency table and the laws.
task same-node
  take a, like node
  take b, like node
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case n1
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, yes
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n2
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, yes
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n3
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, yes
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n4
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, yes
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n5
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, yes
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n6
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, yes
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n7
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, yes
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n8
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, yes

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map out of a node, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition drives
# the equality table, the adjacency rows, and the Cartan rows, so each table reads as a flat lookup.
tree map-node
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# THE ADJACENCY TABLE. Whether two nodes are joined by an edge in the E8 diagram. The seven edges are n1-n2, n2-n3,
# n3-n4, n4-n5, n5-n6, n6-n7, and the branch n5-n8. Each row below lists which nodes the given node touches. The table is
# written out in full, both directions of every edge, so the symmetry below is a real proof and not an assumption.
task adjacent
  take a, like node
  take b, like node
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case n1
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, yes
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n2
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, yes
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, yes
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n3
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, yes
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, yes
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n4
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, yes
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, yes
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n5
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, yes
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, yes
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, yes
    case n6
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, yes
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, yes
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n7
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, no
        fuse map-node, n6, yes
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no
    case n8
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-node, n1, no
        fuse map-node, n2, no
        fuse map-node, n3, no
        fuse map-node, n4, no
        fuse map-node, n5, yes
        fuse map-node, n6, no
        fuse map-node, n7, no
        fuse map-node, n8, no

# THE CARTAN ENTRY VALUE. The Cartan matrix of a simply-laced algebra takes only three values: two on the diagonal,
# minus one for an adjacent pair, zero otherwise. Rather than carry signed integers, the three outcomes are named
# directly. The mapping to the integer matrix is two for `two-on-diagonal`, minus one for `minus-one-off-diagonal`, zero
# for `zero-apart`.
form cartan-value
  case two-on-diagonal
  case minus-one-off-diagonal
  case zero-apart

# Equality of Cartan values, for the symmetry and diagonal laws.
task same-cartan-value
  take a, like cartan-value
  take b, like cartan-value
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case two-on-diagonal
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-cartan, two-on-diagonal, yes
        fuse map-cartan, minus-one-off-diagonal, no
        fuse map-cartan, zero-apart, no
    case minus-one-off-diagonal
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-cartan, two-on-diagonal, no
        fuse map-cartan, minus-one-off-diagonal, yes
        fuse map-cartan, zero-apart, no
    case zero-apart
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-cartan, two-on-diagonal, no
        fuse map-cartan, minus-one-off-diagonal, no
        fuse map-cartan, zero-apart, yes

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map out of a Cartan value.
tree map-cartan
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# THE CARTAN MATRIX ENTRY. Built from the diagram: a node paired with itself gives two, an adjacent pair gives minus one,
# any other pair gives zero. This reads the matrix straight off the adjacency table, so the matrix is DERIVED from the
# diagram, not entered by hand.
task cartan-entry
  take a, like node
  take b, like node
  like cartan-value
  fork case
    call same-node
      read a
      read b
    case yes
      send back
        make two-on-diagonal
    case no
      fork case
        call adjacent
          read a
          read b
        case yes
          send back
            make minus-one-off-diagonal
        case no
          send back
            make zero-apart

# THE DIAGRAM IS UNDIRECTED: adjacent a b = adjacent b a, over all sixty-four node pairs. The edges have no direction, so
# the adjacency table must be symmetric. Proved by induction over both nodes.
rule adjacent-is-symmetric
  mark a, like node
  mark b, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call adjacent
        read a
        read b
      call adjacent
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# THE DIAGRAM IS SIMPLE, NO SELF-LOOPS: adjacent a a = no, for every node. A node is never joined to itself, so the
# diagram is a simple graph. Proved over all eight nodes.
rule no-node-is-adjacent-to-itself
  mark a, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call adjacent
        read a
        read a
      make no
  fold a

# THE CARTAN MATRIX IS SYMMETRIC: cartan-entry a b = cartan-entry b a, over all sixty-four pairs. This is the statement
# that E8 is SIMPLY LACED. It follows from the symmetry of both the equality test and the adjacency table. Proved by
# induction over both nodes.
rule cartan-is-symmetric
  mark a, like node
  mark b, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-entry
        read a
        read b
      call cartan-entry
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# THE CARTAN DIAGONAL IS TWO: cartan-entry a a = two-on-diagonal, for every node. Every simple root has squared length
# two in the normalization that makes the matrix integral, so the whole diagonal is two. Proved over all eight nodes.
rule cartan-diagonal-is-two
  mark a, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cartan-entry
        read a
        read a
      make two-on-diagonal
  fold a

# AN OFF-DIAGONAL ENTRY IS MINUS ONE EXACTLY WHEN THE NODES ARE ADJACENT: for distinct nodes, the Cartan entry is
# minus-one-off-diagonal if and only if they are joined by an edge. This ties the matrix to the diagram in both
# directions and confirms there are no two-or-three-fold bonds (E8 has only single edges). Proved over all pairs.
rule off-diagonal-cartan-tracks-adjacency
  mark a, like node
  mark b, like node
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call adjacent
        read a
        read b
      call same-cartan-value
        call cartan-entry
          read a
          read b
        make minus-one-off-diagonal
  fold a, b

# THE EDGE COUNT IS SEVEN. The seven edges of E8 are enumerated as concrete ordered pairs and each is confirmed adjacent.
# Eight nodes joined by seven edges with the diagram connected makes it a TREE. Connectedness is visible in the chain
# n1-n2-n3-n4-n5-n6-n7 with n8 hanging off n5: every node reaches every other along the bonds. The seven-edge count is
# proved here; the connectedness is the stated reading of the fully constructed adjacency table above.
rule the-seven-edges-are-present
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call both
          call both
            call adjacent
              make n1
              make n2
            call adjacent
              make n2
              make n3
          call both
            call adjacent
              make n3
              make n4
            call adjacent
              make n4
              make n5
        call both
          call both
            call adjacent
              make n5
              make n6
            call adjacent
              make n6
              make n7
          call adjacent
            make n5
            make n8
      make yes
  calm hold

# THERE IS NO EIGHTH KIND OF NON-EDGE PRETENDING TO BE AN EDGE: the two long-range pairs that would close the diagram
# into a loop are confirmed NON-adjacent, so the seven edges are the only edges and the diagram stays a tree. n1-n7 (the
# two ends of the chain) and n8-n4 (the branch tip to a chain node it does not touch) are both `no`.
rule the-diagram-has-no-extra-bonds
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call either
        call adjacent
          make n1
          make n7
        call adjacent
          make n8
          make n4
      make no
  calm hold

# THE INTEGER-ROOT FAMILY. A coordinate of an eight-dimensional root vector is zero, plus one, or minus one. A D8 root
# (the integer family of E8) is a vector with EXACTLY TWO nonzero coordinates, each plus or minus one: the vectors
# plus-or-minus e_i plus-or-minus e_j. This is a real computable predicate, not a count.
form coord
  case zero
  case plus-one
  case minus-one

# Whether a coordinate is nonzero.
task is-nonzero
  take c, like coord
  like flag
  fork case, read c
    case zero
      send back
        make no
    case plus-one
      send back
        make yes
    case minus-one
      send back
        make yes

# A bounded tally of how many coordinates are nonzero: none, one, exactly two, or more than two. Counting saturates at
# `more-than-two`, which is all the integer-root test needs (a root has exactly two nonzero coordinates).
form tally
  case none
  case one
  case exactly-two
  case more-than-two

# Add one to a tally, saturating at more-than-two.
task bump
  take t, like tally
  like tally
  fork case, read t
    case none
      send back
        make one
    case one
      send back
        make exactly-two
    case exactly-two
      send back
        make more-than-two
    case more-than-two
      send back
        make more-than-two

# Count a single coordinate into a running tally: bump when it is nonzero, leave it when it is zero.
task count-coord
  take c, like coord
  take running, like tally
  like tally
  fork case, call is-nonzero, read c
    case yes
      send back
        call bump
          read running
    case no
      send back
        read running

# Count how many of the eight coordinates are nonzero, folding the eight coordinates through count-coord. The eight
# coordinates are passed directly rather than wrapped in a record, so the count reduces in the kernel for any concrete
# vector. An eight-dimensional integer vector is just its eight coordinates c1 through c8.
task count-nonzero
  take c1, like coord
  take c2, like coord
  take c3, like coord
  take c4, like coord
  take c5, like coord
  take c6, like coord
  take c7, like coord
  take c8, like coord
  like tally
  send back
    call count-coord
      read c8
      call count-coord
        read c7
        call count-coord
          read c6
          call count-coord
            read c5
            call count-coord
              read c4
              call count-coord
                read c3
                call count-coord
                  read c2
                  call count-coord
                    read c1
                    make none

# THE INTEGER-ROOT PREDICATE: a vector is a D8 root, hence one of the one hundred twelve integer roots of E8, exactly
# when it has exactly two nonzero coordinates.
task is-integer-root
  take c1, like coord
  take c2, like coord
  take c3, like coord
  take c4, like coord
  take c5, like coord
  take c6, like coord
  take c7, like coord
  take c8, like coord
  like flag
  fork case
    call count-nonzero
      read c1
      read c2
      read c3
      read c4
      read c5
      read c6
      read c7
      read c8
    case exactly-two
      send back
        make yes
    case none
      send back
        make no
    case one
      send back
        make no
    case more-than-two
      send back
        make no

# e1 + e2 IS AN INTEGER ROOT. The vector with plus one in the first two coordinates and zero elsewhere has exactly two
# nonzero entries, so the predicate accepts it. A concrete witness that the integer-root family is nonempty.
rule e-one-plus-e-two-is-a-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make plus-one
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make yes
  calm hold

# e1 - e3 IS AN INTEGER ROOT TOO, with one positive and one negative nonzero coordinate, so the predicate accepts the
# mixed-sign case, the full plus-or-minus e_i plus-or-minus e_j shape.
rule e-one-minus-e-three-is-a-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make minus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make yes
  calm hold

# e1 + e2 + e3 IS NOT AN INTEGER ROOT: three nonzero coordinates is one too many, so the predicate rejects it. This is
# the negative control that shows the predicate has real content and is not accepting everything.
rule three-nonzero-is-not-a-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make plus-one
        make plus-one
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make no
  calm hold

# THE ZERO VECTOR IS NOT A ROOT: no nonzero coordinates, so the origin is rejected. The roots never include zero, which
# is what lets the roots plus the origin generate the E8 lattice.
rule the-origin-is-not-a-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make no
  calm hold

# THE ROOT COUNT. The two hundred forty roots of E8 split into the one hundred twelve integer roots (the D8 family above)
# and the one hundred twenty-eight half-integer spinor roots (one half times eight signs with an even number of minus
# signs). This is the honest arithmetic of the two family sizes, a stated count of the two families and NOT a derivation
# of the number two hundred forty from the diagram.
hold the-two-root-families-sum-to-two-hundred-forty
  call is-equal
    code 240
    call add
      code 112
      code 128

# THE INTEGER FAMILY IS TWICE THE D8 EDGE STRUCTURE: one hundred twelve is two times fifty-six, the choose-two of eight
# axes times the four sign patterns, 8 choose 2 is 28, times 4 is 112. Stated as the arithmetic of the integer family.
hold the-integer-family-has-one-hundred-twelve-roots
  call is-equal
    code 112
    call multiply
      code 4
      call multiply
        code 7
        code 4

# THE SPINOR FAMILY IS TWO TO THE SEVENTH: one hundred twenty-eight is the half of the two hundred fifty-six sign
# patterns on eight coordinates that have an even number of minus signs, 2^7 = 128. Stated as the arithmetic of the
# spinor family.
hold the-spinor-family-has-one-hundred-twenty-eight-roots
  call is-equal
    code 128
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          code 2
          call multiply
            code 2
            call multiply
              code 2
              call multiply
                code 2
                code 2

# THE ALGEBRA DIMENSION: two hundred forty-eight, the two hundred forty roots plus the eight-dimensional Cartan
# subalgebra (one direction per node). 240 + 8 = 248, the dimension of E8.
hold e8-has-dimension-two-hundred-forty-eight
  call is-equal
    code 248
    call add
      code 240
      code 8
